\layout Subsection*

Global Balance
\layout Standard

We seek a stationary distribution by looking for global balance equations.
 Global balance equations state that at equilibrium the frequency of transitions
 out of state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 equals the frequency of transitions into 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 (pg 260-262 Bertsekas and Gallager).
 
\layout Subsubsection*

Discrete Time Markov Chain
\layout Standard

In its most general form a discrete time Markov process can be defined by
 a transition probability matrix
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{P}=\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
 P_{11} & \cdots  & P_{i1} & \cdots  & P_{N1}\\
 \vdots  & \ddots  &  &  & \vdots \\
 P_{1j} &  & P_{ij} &  & P_{Nj}\\
 \vdots  &  &  & \ddots  & \vdots \\
 P_{1N} & \cdots  & P_{iN} & \cdots  & P_{NN}\end{array}
\right]\]

\end_inset 

if the steady state probability of being in state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $P(j)$
\end_inset 

 then the global balance equations state
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum _{i=0}^{\infty }P_{ji}=\sum _{i=0}^{\infty }P\left(i\right)P_{ij}\]

\end_inset 

which in English says the probability of leaving state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is equal to the probability of arriving in state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsubsection*

Continuous Time Markov Chain
\layout Standard

In continuous time Markov chains we are interested in rates of transfer
 rather than probabilities if the rates of transfer between states are given
 by a matrix whose elements 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 give the rate of transfer between state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

, then the global balance equations give
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum _{i=0}^{\infty }q_{ji}=\sum _{i=0}^{\infty }P\left(i\right)q_{ij}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Detailed Balance
\layout Standard

For the birth death processes we are considering transitions only occur
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

.
 This leads us to a special case of the global balance equations known as
 the detailed balance equations which look at the flow between two states.
 For the discrete case
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)P_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)P_{k,k-1}\]

\end_inset 

and for the continuous case 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)q_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)q_{k,k-1}\]

\end_inset 

however in our notation the rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu _{k}$
\end_inset 

 and the rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda _{k-1}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard


\begin_inset Include \input{./diagrams/boundaryFlow.eepic}

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a Boundary.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Thus at steady state, flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 = 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda _{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu _{k}P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 For flow to be balanced we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda _{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)=\mu _{k}P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k)=\frac{\lambda _{k-1}}{\mu _{k}}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Subsection*


\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

