\layout Section

Average Delay
\layout Standard

Little's formula tells us that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=\lambda \bar{T}$
\end_inset 

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}$
\end_inset 

 is the average delay.
 This gives us the average delay as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
T=\frac{1}{\mu -\lambda }\]

\end_inset 

 This is not the time spent in the queue however, this is the time spent
 in the system which includes 
\emph on 
propagation delay
\emph default 
.
 
\layout Subsection*

Little's formula for Queue
\layout Standard

We can apply Little's formula again to find the number in the queue.
 
\layout Standard

The rate for this version is the same, but we are now interested in the
 average number in the queue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

, the average waiting time, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}$
\end_inset 

, is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{T}-\frac{1}{\mu }=\frac{1}{\mu -\lambda }-\frac{1}{\mu }\]

\end_inset 

 The rate of arrivals will be the same, so the average number in the queue
 will be 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda \bar{W}=\frac{\rho ^{2}}{1-\rho }\]

\end_inset 


